our next question today is from Roger  who says should being an organ donor be  mandatory the so this is an interesting  question because being an organ donor is  optional there are varying opinions  about whether you should be opting in or  out in other words the default be that  everyone's an organ donor unless they  opt out or should default the default be  what it is now which is you are not an  organ donor unless you opt in one of the  more common reasons that people site  against organ donation is because of  freedom of religion you cannot mandate  organ donation since it would go against  the religious beliefs of some religions  and as by its very nature you would have  to at minimum grant religious exemptions  and that caused us a whole other set of  issues freedom of choice is a more  common reason which is were as  individuals were not under an obligation  to allow our bodies to be used to  potentially save other people even  though you could argue that it would be  from from a sort of overall well-being  perspective a better choice that we are  not we have freedom of choice when it  comes to that and slippery slope  arguments are also made which are hey if  you mandate organ donation there could  be all sorts of other unintended  consequences for example groups that go  out and murder people in particular ways  knowing that they could be sort of the  Robin Hood's of organs taking from a  healthy person and maybe saving five or  six unhealthy people I reject that  argument because murder is still against  the law and and we sort of have a  mechanism by which you're not allowed to  just go out and and murder people  there's also a population argument  that's made individuals who are very  skittish about any action which would  drastically accelerate population growth  say you know there's sort of a natural  population limiting effect of only a  fraction of the population  being organ donors and if all of a  sudden every time someone dies you're  potentially saving the lives of multiple  people that is going to put significant  strain on our planet in terms of  resources etc and then there's also the  argument against about medical  malpractice which is there's actually  one of those basic medical ethics  problems the trolley problem as compared  to healthy person comes into the  hospital do you kill them to save five  of your patients that are about to die  if they don't get organ transplants I  actually think Pat that most of those  arguments against are hypothetical and  speculative in a way that doesn't really  address the issue I think the main issue  is that in our current understanding of  society there is no legal justification  for mandating organ donation there's  just i've not seen if you're going to  say organ donation should be mandated  then you need to be able to demonstrate  where the government would get the power  to mandate such a default action and  I've not seen a compelling case that the  government would be within its legal  rights to do that right especially I  think the religious argument is probably  the most sound there because you know  people have a rights to their bodies and  especially if they think there's a  religious reason as to why they have to  keep their organs intact I think that  would hold up quite well in court as to  keep the system the way it is but also  when you die do you really have the same  rights to your body as you do when  you're alive because obviously you can't  control it other people have to pick up  the mess there I think the most  interesting thing you mentioned there  though is do we have the same rights to  our body when we die is when we're alive  because the first argument you made the  religious exemption argument if you can  determine that a certain action is  paramount to public health like for  example the vaccination of kids you can  say as California has said that there  will not be exemptions granted for  children looking to attend public school  for vaccinations on the basis of  religion if you don't want to have your  kids vaccinated they cannot go to public  school and you could make the same  argument that you made just then Pat  about a religious reason not to have  organ donation happen the difference for  me the key difference that you brought  up is do we have the same right to our  body when we die and that is a much more  complicated question because when alive  mandating vaccinations in spite or  despite religious preferences  contradicts the argument you made but  what what is our right to our body when  we die and I think if there is a legal  case to be made for the government being  able to mandate organ donation  my sense is it comes somewhere along  those lines so I think that's a super  super interesting approach and an  interesting way to think about it and to  answer Rodgers question right now I  don't believe that organ donation should  be mandatory
 

